The sum is: \[
\sum_{i=0}^{\infty} a_i^2 = \frac{a_0^2}{1 - r_a^2}
\]

Thus, the sum is: \[
\sum_{i=0}^{\infty} a_i b_i = \frac{a_0 b_0}{1 - r_a r_b}
\]

Putting this all together, the given condition is: \[
\left( \frac{a_0^2}{1 - r_a^2} \right) \left( \frac{b_0^2}{1 - r_b^2} \right) = \frac{a_0 b_0}{1 - r_a r_b}
\]

Right-hand side (RHS): \[
\frac{a_0 b_0}{1 - (1 - a_0)(1 - b_0)}
\]

Therefore, LHS becomes: \[
\left( \frac{a_0^2}{a_0(2 - a_0)} \right) \left( \frac{b_0^2}{b_0(2 - b_0)} \right) = \left( \frac{a_0}{2 - a_0} \right) \left( \frac{b_0}{2 - b_0} \right) = \frac{a_0 b_0}{(2 - a_0)(2 - b_0)}
\]

Thus, RHS is: \[
\frac{a_0 b_0}{a_0 + b_0 - a_0 b_0}
\]

So putting LHS and RHS together: \[
\frac{a_0 b_0}{(2 - a_0)(2 - b_0)} = \frac{a_0 b_0}{a_0 + b_0 - a_0 b_0}
\]

Since \( a_0 \) and \( b_0 \) are positive (from the earlier constraints \( 0 < a_0, b_0 < 2 \)), we can divide both sides by \( a_0 b_0 \), getting: \[
\frac{1}{(2 - a_0)(2 - b_0)} = \frac{1}{a_0 + b_0 - a_0 b_0}
\]
